## ***Historical Context***

Amos prophesied during the mid-Eighth Century BC. This was a time of great prosperity for both the Northern Kingdom (Israel) and the Southern Kingdom (Judah). This caused the citizens to confuse economic comfort with Divine endorsement. Amos was a shepherd and sycamore tree farmer from a small rural town near the Judaian Wilderness. But as a Southerner, he was sent to the Northern Kingdom. He went to the religiously important Northern city of Bethel. This is one of the two places where the first king of the Northern Kingdom, Jeroboam I, built a temple and placed a golden calf (see Exodus 32 and I Kings 12) to draw his citizens away from true worship at the Temple in Jerusalem.

## ***The Two Witnesses***&nbsp;

Amos was a contemporary of Hosea. So, why does God have two prophets saying basically the same thing?&nbsp;

Throughout Scripture there is the necessity for at least two witnesses to condemn someone. It first appears in the Law (Deut. 17 and 19). The same requirement is given numerous times in the New Testament (Matt 18:16; 2 Cor. 13:1; 1 Tim 5:19: Heb 10:28). God provided the two witnesses that had the same message of condemnation and restoration, but each had their unique style of presentation.&nbsp;

## ***The Roar - Aslan is on the Move***

Amos opens with the image of the LORD’s roar echoing across the landscape. It isn't only heard in the capital city but in the farmlands and mountains. It affects everyone and everything. It brings to mind two quotes from C.S. Lewis in The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe.

*“They say Aslan is on the move—perhaps has already landed.”*

*and,*

*“Wrong will be right, when Aslan comes in sight, At the sound of his roar, sorrows will be no more, When he bares his teeth, winter meets its death, And when he shakes his mane, we shall have spring again.”*

## ***The Trap***

In the movie, *The Hunger Games* (full disclosure—I have not seen any Hunger Games movies, nor read the books), the privileged society in the capital view those in the Outer Districts as inferior, as morally compromised.

*"At least have some manners! The pair last year ate everything with their hands like a couple of savages. It completely upset my digestion."* Effie Trinket, the official in charge of Outer District 12.

In the movie, those from the Capital cheer the destruction of those from the Outer Districts.

The Hunger Games could have been written by Amos! The citizens of both the Kingdoms of Judah and Israel had the same sentiment as the citizens of the Capital in *The Hunger Games*.&nbsp;

- *Jeremiah 7:4-10 "We have the Temple; we are safe!"*
- *Luke 18:9-14 "Thank God I am not like other people."*
- *Romans 2:17-24 "We have the Law; we are guides to the blind."*

Amos sets a trap for his audience by first issuing condemnation to six nations that surrounded the&nbsp; Kingdoms of Israel and Judah. You can just hear his audience cheering for the destruction of these inferior nations who were their enemies.

&nbsp;You can almost hear them saying, *“It's about time! Called it!”*&nbsp;

But they started cheering too soon! Because God, in 2:4 condemns Judah and two verses later He condemns Israel for crimes committed. Just when Amos’ audience is nodding and cheering him on, He circles the Kingdoms with His condemnations on the surrounding nations until His finger is pointing right at them.&nbsp;

It's like Nathan confronting King David about his adulterous relationship with Bathsheba. Nathan traps David by telling him the story of an unjust rich man and gets David to utter the angry condemnation. “*As the* LORD *lives, the man who has done this deserves to die!*” (2 Samuel 12)

Nathan closes the trap by proclaiming, “You are the man!”

You can almost see Amos thinking of this incident when he sets up his audience in Bethel!

## ***The Three Plus Four Formula - Beyond a Shadow of Doubt***&nbsp;

Amos uses a literary phrase for each of his condemnations.&nbsp;

*“For three transgressions,...and for four.”*

He's saying that the sum is seven, or complete. “*Your sins are more than enough to condemn you.*”

This same type of metaphor is used positively in Job 5:19:\
\*He will deliver you from six troubles; in seven no evil shall touch you (*His care is complete, or full).

and negatively in Proverbs 6:16:

*There are six things that the* LORD *hates, seven that are an abomination to him:* (His hatred of these sins is complete, or full).

The number itself is not important. It’s the conveyance of totality, or completeness. We could use a similar metaphor by saying something like:&nbsp;

*“There are a dozen reasons this won't work—actually, make that thirteen."*

Another illustration of what Amos is saying is the message that the Hand on the Wall wrote for Belshazzar (Daniel 5).

***Mene, Mene, Tekel, Peres***&nbsp;

Daniel interpreted the writing by saying:

“This is the interpretation of the matter: ***Mene***\*, God has numbered the days of your kingdom and brought it to an end;* ***Tekel***\*, you have been weighed in the balances and found wanting;*&nbsp; ***Peres****, your kingdom is divided and given to the Medes and Persians.”* Daniel 5:26-28

You have been found guilty beyond a shadow of doubt.&nbsp;

## &nbsp;***"I will not turn back.": (Lo Ashivenhu)***

After they have been found guilty beyond a shadow of doubt, comes the appeal. In the context of Amos 1:3–2:5, the phrase *"I will not turn back"* (***lo ashivenhu***) is the divine equivalent of the Supreme Court issuing that final order upholding the capital penalty: *"Application for stay denied.*"&nbsp;

This is a chilling phrase coming from the God of the Universe, the LORD of Hosts. Throughout the Prophets the concepts of ***hesed*** (covenant love/mercy)***, metanict/nacham*** (relenting or changing course), an***d arech apayim*** ("slow to anger,") are prevalent. They demonstrate the legal equivalent of a restraining order, but one that God has placed on Himself. Now however, it is all over. He lifts His restraining order.

It brings to mind Julius Caesar crossing the Rubicon (***Alea iacta est***): When Caesar uttered "The die is cast" in 49 BC, it became the ancient world's ultimate idiom for *****lo ashivenhu****—*a irreversible threshold crossed where legal debate ends and execution begins.

## ***The Application: Escaping the Finger-Pointing Trap Today***

I am very good at seeing what others do wrong! I think we all are. We are just as good as the people of Israel and Judah at saying, *“It's about time! Called it!”* But God speaks to us just like he did to David, “*You are the man!”* Somehow we end up in the same trap as those who were listening to Amos in Bethel. We point fingers at others and then find ourselves in the same trap as they found themselves. We are forced to look into the mirror of our souls and see that we are just like the citizens of the Capital Districts, like the Syrians, the Philistines, the Ammonites, the Edomites….

To quote John Mark Green, American poet and writer:

*"The self-righteous scream judgments against others to hide the noise of skeletons dancing in their own closets."*

*Or, better yet, Jesus said:*\
*"Why do you look at the speck of sawdust in your brother’s eye and pay no attention to the plank in your own eye?"* Matthew 7:3

We abandon the moral high ground when we cheer for the destruction of the “outgroup” because the moral rot is right there inside of us.

*“Wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me from this body of death? Thanks be to God through Jesus Christ our* LORD*! So then, I myself serve the law of God with my mind, but with my flesh I serve the law of sin.”* Romans 7:24-25
